* This file

This file attempts to describe the hacking guidelines followed in
Heroes.  We try to follow the GNU standards (with some slight
modifications as it will be noted below) and most of their guidelines
are not repeated here.  Therefore we recommend the reading of
standards.info, available on every good GNU system.

Please keep in mind that these are GUIDELINES, not MILITARY ORDERS!
Anything can be discussed.

Questions or suggestions about this file can be sent to
<heroes-discuss@lists.sourceforge.net>.


* Developer tools used in Heroes

Required to bootstrap a CVS checkout:

 autoconf 2.49a  (from alpha.gnu.org)
 automake 1.4a   (current CVS version)
 gperf		 (from ftp.gnu.org)

Optionally needed if you change some specific parts of the game:

 help2man	 (http://www.ozemail.com.au/~bod)
 texinfo	 (from ftp.gnu.org)
 zip		 (ftp://ftp.cdrom.com/pub/infozip/src/zip23.tar.gz)
 unzip		 (ftp://ftp.cdrom.com/pub/infozip/src/unzip540.tar.gz)

Required to commit a patch if you have write access to the CVS server:

 cvs-utils	 (from subversions.gnu.org)


* Sending patches

If you patched Heroes and want to have your patch included in a next
release, please send it to <heroes-discuss@lists.sourceforge.net>.
(This is a general discussion list, there is presently no list
dedicated to patches.)


* General coding conventions

** Formatting

Function definitions (in .c files) are written with their name on the
first column, return type and linkage specifiers are left on the line
above.

  static int
  foo (const char* bar)
  {
    /* ... */
  }

This allow to detect function definitions easily (should it be needed
to write such a script somewhen in future) since `grep ^foo *.c' is an
easy mean to locate a function definition.

Functions declarations (in .h or sometimes .c files) can be written on
a single line, we don't really care.

  static int fubar (void);

We use a two spaces indentations, as the GNU standards; but we keep
brackets on the same line as the flow-control keywords (if, switch,
for, etc.), like in the Linux kernel.

  do {
    if (cond2) {
      /* ... */
    } else if (cond3) {
      /* ... */
    } else {
      /* ... */
    }
  } while (cond1);

`-br' and `-ce' are the options to give to `indent' to get this
formatting -- but the use of `indent' on existing files is
discouraged: in some cases the output is uglier than the original.

** Includes

Any .c file should include "system.h" as the first included file.
Read the beginning of system.h for more explanations.  Any .h
can assume system.h has already been included, but should include
other local headers needed (e.g. type declaration used in function
prototypes) so that inclusion order is not important.

** FIXME

The keywords `FIXME:', `NOTE:', and `WARNING:' can be used to
emphasis any comment in the sources.  Try to make the important part
of your comment fit on the same line of the keyword, such that `grep
FIXME: *.[ch]' give a nice summary.

** ChangeLog

Any change to a file that goes into the distribution should have a
corresponding ChangeLog entry.  Do NOT abbreviate filenames or
functions names in any way!  A simple search in the ChangeLog should
allow anyone to find the last change to the searched file or function
(this is very helpful the day you try to fix a bug without having
access to the CVS history).

Removals in files BUGS or TODO can be implicit (i.e. without ChangeLog
entries) when the current patch is fixing the corresponding bug or
completing a TODO item.  Idem for additions to THANKS.

Changes to files which are out of the distribution (e.g. .cvsignore
files) don't need any ChangeLog entry.

Always prepend to the ChangeLog, and avoid changing an old ChangeLog
entries.  If you *really* need to modify an old entry, make a separate
patch for this.  Generally speaking it better to make many small
patches rather than one big patch, whenever you work can be cut.

The most accurate the ChangeLog is, the more it is helpful.

** RCS keywords

Do not use them; they make the mixing of files from different projects
complicated.

If you stole a file from another project which has such keywords,
don't remove them, just "cvs add -ko" the file (-ko prevent keywords
substitution).


* Files that should not be changed

Some files are auto-generated, or maintained elsewhere and
should therefore not be modified directly.

** Auto-generated files

Files which will go into the distribution, but are not in the CVS
because they can be generated from files already kept the CVS.

 aclocal.m4		   (generated by aclocal
				      from configure.in and m4/*)
 configure		   (generated by autoconf
				      from configure.in)
 src/config.h.in	   (generated by autoheader
				      from configure.in and aclocal.m4)
 **/Makefile.am		   (generated by automake
				      from configure.in)
 arch/VisualC/VisualC.zip  (generated by arch/Makefile
				      using zip
				      from arch/Visual/*)
 rsc_files_hash.c	   (generated by src/Makefile
				      using gperf
				      from src/rsc_files.gperf)
 doc/heroes.info	   (generated by doc/Makefile
				      using makeinfo
				      from src/heroes.texi)
 man/heroes.6		   (generated by man/Makefile
				      using help2man
				      from the Heroes binary)

** Not generated, but added automtically by automake

 ... and therefore not in CVS.

 COPYING, INSTALL, tools/config.guess, tools/config.sub, tools/depcomp,
 tools/install-sh, tools/mdate-sh, tools/missing, tools/mkinstalldirs,
 tools/texinfo.tex

** Files in CVS, but maintained elsewhere

Avoid any change to these files, if possible.
(And report any change upstream, of course.)

 lib/getopt.c	 (from glibc)
 lib/getopt1.c	 (from glibc)
 lib/gnugetopt.h (from glibc, where it is named getopt.h)

* CVS

** Building from CVS

As generated files are not kept in CVS, you need to generate them
after your first checkout.  The script tools/autogen.sh will take care
of generating the autotools required files.  After you have run
autogen, you will be able to make a first build first build
(./configure; make) to create the documentation and the other files
generated by Makefile rules.

** Committing changes

If you have write access to the CVS, you shall use clcommit to commit
your patches (clcommit can be obtained from the cvs-tools modules of
subversion.gnu.org CVS server).

clcommit will use your ChangeLog entry as a commit message, and complain
if you forgot to make a ChangeLog entry.

Exceptional cases where clcommit is not used:
  - if your patch is a modification of some prior entry (you would
    otherwise fool clcommit which use diff to extract the commit message).
  - you cannot have a ChangeLog entry for the patch (because the ChangeLog
    entry is already in CVS, you just forgot to "cvs add" the file; or
    you have changed a non-distribued file, such as .cvsignore, not
    even worth to mention in a ChangeLog).
  - you are merging a CVS branch

** Tracking other changes

CVS commit are automatically sent as patches, to
heroes-commits@lists.sourceforge.net.  You can
subscribe to this list using the following page.
http://lists.sourceforge.net/mailman/listinfo/heroes-commits

** CVS write access

When you have send some patches for Heroes, want to further contribute
to the game, and would prefer to commit to the CVS directly, just ask!

The present policy is to commit most changes BEFORE discussing (if
needed).  Most of the time there is nothing to discuss, therefore it
saves time.  Moreover, it forces people to put important comments
in the code (it would otherwise be tempting to document something on
the mailling list and not in the code).

But this put more responsibility on the people who commit
patches, therefore if you don't feel confident you can still send your
patches to the list for review before committing them.

** Committing patches from other people

If there is no ChangeLog entry, write one (and add a "From <xxx@yyy>"
comment line) or ask the author to make one.  Don't forget to put
the author in THANKS.

** Bug fixes

If you fix a bug reported by someone, add a "Reported by
<xxx@yyy>" line to your ChangeLog entry.  Put him in THANKS too.

** Tag and branches

The main trunk should remain the latest, bleeding edge, development
version of the game.

Releases are tagged using symbolic name such as heroes_0_5 (for heroes-0.5)
or heroes_0_6b (for heroes-0.6b).

After a public release there is usually an errata branch
(e.g. heroes_0_6_errata) created, and rooted on the corresponding
release version.  This branch is for important bug fixes (e.g. bugs
that prevent the game to compile or run).  Every patch commited to
that branch should be merged in the main trunk.


* Release process

** Before a public release

 - Update externally-maintained files
 - Update administrative files:
   + README must be updated from heroes.texi
      (run `makeinfo --no-headers -o - heroes.texi', cut and paste the
       relevant sections)
   + THANKS (all the emails from the ChangeLog should
             figure in THANKS or AUTHORS)
   + NEWS,TODO,BUGS must be up to date
 - Set version number in configure.in
 - make a dummy build (to update the generated file to the new version)
 - build the distribution using `make distcheck'

If the above was successful:

 - commit the changes to CVS
 - tag the repository (cvs tag heroes_xx_yy)

